[QllickFizz | developer] Sure, I can help with that. Let's create a format for organizing and generating content with specific sections for context, constants, and content data. Here’s a structured format in YAML, which can be easily parsed and used in your project.

### Content Organization Format

```yaml
# Context Section
context:
  # General context information
  topic: "Example Topic"
  description: "A brief description of the topic."
  metadata:
    created_at: "2023-10-01T12:00:00Z"
    updated_at: "2023-10-01T12:00:00Z"
    author: "Denis"

# Constants Section
constants:
  # Define any constants or placeholder values
  base_url: "https://example.com"
  api_key: "your_api_key_here"
  default_language: "en"

# Content Data Section
content:
  # Main content data
  title: "Example Title"
  body: "This is the main content of the document."
  sections:
    - title: "Section 1"
      content: "Content of section 1."
    - title: "Section 2"
      content: "Content of section 2."
      images:
        - url: "https://example.com/image1.jpg"
          alt: "Image 1"
        - url: "https://example.com/image2.jpg"
          alt: "Image 2"
  tags:
    - "tag1"
    - "tag2"
    - "tag3"
```

### Explanation

1. **Context Section**: 
   - `topic`: The main topic or subject of the content.
   - `description`: A brief description of the topic.
   - `metadata`: Additional metadata such as creation and update timestamps, author, etc.

2. **Constants Section**: 
   - `base_url`: Base URL for any relative paths or API calls.
   - `api_key`: API key for authentication, if needed.
   - `default_language`: Default language for the content.

3. **Content Data Section**: 
   - `title`: The title of the content.
   - `body`: The main body of the content.
   - `sections`: An array of sections, each with a title and content. Sections can also include images.
   - `tags`: An array of tags for categorization.

### Usage

You can use this format to structure your content in a consistent and organized manner. Here’s an example of how you might use this in a Node.js project:

```typescript
import * as fs from 'fs';
import * as yaml from 'yaml';

interface Content {
  context: {
    topic: string;
    description: string;
    metadata: {
      created_at: string;
      updated_at: string;
      author: string;
    };
  };
  constants: {
    base_url: string;
    api_key: string;
    default_language: string;
  };
  content: {
    title: string;
    body: string;
    sections: {
      title: string;
      content: string;
      images?: {
        url: string;
        alt: string;
      }[];
    }[];
    tags: string[];
  };
}

const contentData: Content = {
  context: {
    topic: "Example Topic",
    description: "A brief description of the topic.",
    metadata: {
      created_at: "2023-10-01T12:00:00Z",
      updated_at: "2023-10-01T12:00:00Z",
      author: "Denis"
    }
  },
  constants: {
    base_url: "https://example.com",
    api_key: "your_api_key_here",
    default_language: "en"
  },
  content: {
    title: "Example Title",
    body: "This is the main content of the document.",
    sections: [
      {
        title: "Section 1",
        content: "Content of section 1."
      },
      {
        title: "Section 2",
        content: "Content of section 2.",
        images: [
          {
            url: "https://example.com/image1.jpg",
            alt: "Image 1"
          },
          {
            url: "https://example.com/image2.jpg",
            alt: "Image 2"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    tags: ["tag1", "tag2", "tag3"]
  }
};

// Write the content to a YAML file
fs.writeFileSync('content.yaml', yaml.stringify(contentData));
```

This format and example should help you organize and generate content in a structured and consistent manner. Let me know if you need any further assistance!
